==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 01 — PING, TRACEROUTE & MTR
Commands Reference Sheet
==========================================

--- INSTALL (if missing) ---
which ping traceroute mtr
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y traceroute mtr

--- PING ---
ping -c 4 <host>                # send exactly 4 packets, then stop
ping -c 4 127.0.0.1             # loopback: tests your own network stack
ping -c 4 <gateway-ip>          # tests your local network / router
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8               # tests internet reachability (by IP)
ping -c 4 google.com            # tests reachability + DNS (by name)
ping -c 4 -i 0.5 <host>         # 0.5 second interval between packets
ping -c 4 -s 1200 <host>        # bigger packet size (bytes)
ping -c 3 -W 1 <host>           # wait only 1 second per reply (timeout)

--- FINDING YOUR GATEWAY ---
ip route | grep default         # the IP after "via" is your gateway

--- TRACEROUTE ---
traceroute <host>               # trace the path to a host
traceroute -n <host>            # -n = don't resolve names (faster)
traceroute -I <host>            # use ICMP instead of UDP
sudo traceroute -T -p 443 <host> # use TCP port 443 (helps when UDP/ICMP is blocked)

--- MTR ---
mtr <host>                      # live, continuously updating view (q to quit)
mtr -r -c 10 <host>             # report mode: 10 packets, then print and exit
mtr -rn -c 10 <host>            # report mode, numeric (no name lookup)

--- READING THE OUTPUT ---
time=      round-trip latency in milliseconds
ttl=       hops left (64 Linux/macOS, 128 Windows, 255 network devices)
Loss%      % of probes that got no reply
Snt        number of probes sent
Avg/Best/Wrst   average / best / worst latency
* * *      hop did not reply (may just be blocked, not always a problem)

--- QUICK DIAGNOSIS ---
ping 8.8.8.8 OK  +  ping google.com FAIL   ->  DNS problem
ping gateway FAIL                           ->  local network problem
ping gateway OK  +  ping 8.8.8.8 FAIL       ->  problem beyond your router / ISP

